 of force by  police officers shoving was sentenced to  22 and a half years in prison all four  officers are awaiting sentencing for the  federal charges  coming up on law and crime daily  convicted murderers will soon learn  their new fate as jury selection begins  in the re-sentencing trial of alan wade  and michael jackson plus ryan duke is  acquitted of killing but now faces new  charges in a neighboring georgian county  in relation to the death of tara  grinstead  [Music]  welcome back jury selection begins in  the trial of a florida woman accused of  stabbing her husband to death in their  home in 2019. danielle redlich faces  second-degree murder charges and  tampering with evidence charges in the  stabbing death of her husband michael  redlich investigators say redlich called  911 initially telling dispatchers her  husband had a heart attack after the two  had an altercation in 911 calls she  claimed michael stabbed himself and that  she woke up next to him deceased but  investigators believe michael's wounds  were not self-inflicted and that  danielle tried to clean up the crime  scene before calling 9-1-1 the  65-year-old was previously married to  danielle's mother while she suffered and  eventually succumbed to cancer he also  worked as the director of the university  of central florida sports business  management program prosecutors offered  danielle a plea deal to plead guilty to  manslaughter charges two years ago but  she rejected it on monday the judge read  danielle's charges to the group of  prospective jurors  this is the state of florida versus  danielle justine redlich  it charges count number one  second-degree murder with a firearm  count two tampering with physical  evidence  i misspoke  it's second degree murder with a weapon  and then tampering with physical  evidence  it alleges that danielle justine redlich  on the 11th day of january 2019 or the  12th day of january 2019  in orange county florida  by an act imminently dangerous to  another  and evincing a depraved mind regardless  of human life  did kill michael redlich and in the  course of committing said offense  danielle justine redlich did carry  display  use  threaten to use or attempt to use a  weapon  once the jury is selected opening  statements could begin as early as  wednesday  and jury selection begins in another  florida case the death penalty phase  retrial for two men convicted in a 2005  double murder allen wayne and michael  james jackson are two of four people  involved in the deaths of reggie and  carol sumner alan wade michael james  jackson tiffany cole and bruce nixon  were all convicted over a decade ago for  their involvement in the murder cole was  the connection between all the players  involved as she previously had been the  summoner's neighbor before the couple  moved to jacksonville cole argues  jackson hatched the plan to break into  the couple's home while they were away  but that plan evolved into killing them  prosecutors argued at the defendant's  trial that the four dug a hole in a  remote wooded spot in georgia before  kidnapping the sumners the group stole  from reggie and carol's home then forced  them into the trunk of their own car  then in two separate cars the summoners  and a rental the foursome drove reggie  and carroll to the remote location they  forced the couple to share their bank  account information before bearing them  alive wade jackson and cole were all  sentenced to death for the murder nixon  took a plea deal pleading guilty to  second-degree murder now wade in jackson  will be re-sentenced after a change in  florida law which requires a unanimous  jury decision for the death penalty  terry how much of the fact that florida  juries now need to be unanimous to  change this re-sentencing child does it  mean that it's gonna be a lot harder for  the prosecution to secure  a death penalty i guess  you know i don't think it's going to be  harder i think that they're going to  have to put in evidence to show what  exactly occurred and make the jury  understand that this was a heinous crime  and that they're going to have to get a  conviction and it's going to be the  penalty rather is having to be uh  unanimous you can't have not even one  juror say no death penalty so i think  that the prosecution is going to look at  this they're going to make sure that all  the evidence gets in they're going to  see whether or not they have mitigating  facts and you know factors that are  aggravating to make sure that this jury  totally understands that it has to be  unanimous  and for the defense they'll be saying we  just need one now latonya you helped  prosecute the men responsible for the  death of a mod arbory and you've seen  your fair share of trials my  understanding is that we're going to  have two juries here in a single trial  how does that work and why is that going  on  because we have a bifurcated system when  you have the first part of it is the  trial part and you deal with whether or  not somebody's convicted and then you're  going to have the part dealing with the  penalty phase and that's the issue here  is that they're doing a re-sentencing  with regard to the penalty phase whether  or not they actually get the death  penalty or not and so that's why you  have the two different juries  all right we'll see what this jury  decides as they evaluate terry you said  it best the prosecution's gonna present  all that evidence and see how they come  to that conclusion of death or no death  and of course florida laws have changed  requiring that to be unanimous so we'll  keep track on this not that michael  jackson you're probably thinking of  michael james jackson and alan wade the  death penalty charges in out of florida  when we come back new charges for a  georgia man acquitted of murder last  month we'll discuss the case of ryan  dukes and botus charge in relation to  the death and disappearance of tara  grinstead  [Music]  welcome back just two weeks after a  georgia man was acquitted of murdering a  beauty queen and teacher 17 years ago  he's facing new charges in a neighboring  county ryan duke faced an irwin county  jury last month for tara grinstead's  murder but was ultimately charged with  concealing her death duke pointed the  finger at his former friend and  classmate beau dukes who is already  serving 25 years in prison on charges  related to the case grinstead went  missing in 2005 but her disappearance  remained a mystery for more than a  decade at ryan duke's trial last month  he told jurors he lied when he confessed  to the teacher's murder in 2017 but  admitted to helping beau dukes dispose  of her body by burning it in a pecan  orchard at a nearby county duke now  faces six new charges in neighboring  bent hill county including concealing  the death of another hindering  apprehension or punishment of a criminal  concealment of facts and tampering with  evidence in the irwin county case duke  was sentenced to 10 years in prison he  faces more prison time if convicted in  ben hill county beau dukes also faces  charges in ben hill county the trial for  those charges is set to start as early  as the end of june  help us make heads or tales of this how  does this work a defendant serving time  in one county but indicted for the same  death is that in a different county  sorry is that double jeopardy or is that  allowed  i mean it's allowed in certain cases  because you're saying  that  information that happened new  information's come out new evidence has  come out that something actually  happened in this particular county that  venue lies here this is where the actual  crime occurred and what we're talking  about here is specifically about the  concealment of death and that  information came up by him his own  admission on the stand of saying where  it occurred in ben hill county and so  now you've got new charges there based  off of this information right so  probably death in one county concealing  another and that's how they're kind of  splitting it up terry should we be  expecting the same case and same result  in a different county since things are  so similar or is this completely  different in all matters in this the  bent hill county case  you know i think it's going to be a very  similar case because there are very  similar charges two different counties  like you said erwin county where we saw  this case and then ben hill county where  we'll see the second case i think most  of that evidence in the second case is  going to be focusing on like it was in  the first case what occurred after the  murder who handled the body who told who  to do what and i think that basically  the big issue here is the fact that  neither county brought murder charges we  know there was a confession and it seems  to me that the prosecution missed an  opportunity in both counties to bring  charges for murder in this case so i  think that was a big missed opportunity  yeah we'll see how they make out with  that in this case thank you both and  thank you for joining us here on law and  crime daily we'll see you next time as  we discuss justice in america  [Music]  you
 

